## 1. Introduction
The fast development of flexible and wearable electronic devices requires efficient and lightweight miniature power sources[1]. Supercapacitors (SCs) with high power density, fast charge–discharge rate, long cycle life and safety have received increasing attention in energy storage devices[2]. While the relatively low energy density limits the practical application[3]. It is of urgency to fabricate flexible all-solid-state asymmetric SCs (ASCs) to extend the operating potential so as to get high electrochemical performance[3]. Furthermore, seeking highperformance electrode materials is also highly desired. Transition metal sulfide (TMS) has been reported as the effective pseudocapacitive electrode material due to better electrochemical activity than those of the oxides[4]. However, so far the available electrode materials based on TMS still suffer from low rate capability and/or poor electrochemical stability[5–7]. It is promising to design rational nanostructures to enhance its conductivity and suppress its volume expansion. Two-dimensional (2D) nanosheets have demonstrated their merits due to the anisotropic structure and high surface-to-bulk ratio, which grant a shorter diffusion path for electrons and ions[8].
Heteroatomic doping can affect various properties of the samples by influencing the structure[9–15]. On one hand, the cationic doping such as $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ [12–13], ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ [14], ${\sf A g}^{+}$ [14], $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}[14-15]$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ [15] and $C o^{2+}$ [15] can make a more compact structure, smaller particle size[12], and increase the lifetime of electrons and holes [12] and further impact the photodegradation efficiencies[13]. On the other hand, anionic doping is effective in improving the electrochemical properties for altering electron density by inducing new energy level[9]. Among them, phosphorous (P) is considered as an interesting dopant for its lone-pair electron and fully vacant 3d orbitals, which can tune local charge density of adjacent bonded metallic atoms to accommodate the surface charge state and promote surface electroactivity of host materials[10]. Kang et al. reported $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ doping $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ nanotube with enlarged cell, which shows a high specific capacity of $1806.4\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ [11].
Furthermore, defects could also improve the electrochemical performance of electrodes effectively by adjusting their electronic structure while keeping the lattice as a whole largely unchanged [16]. More recently, introducing S vacancies to electrode materials has been reported to enhance their conductivity and electrochemical performance[17]. The embedded electric field could also be induced due to the unbalanced redistribution of charges, which accelerates the migration of ions/electrons[18]. Wang et al. developed S-vacancies in $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ through the plasma introduction, which exhibits much higher capacities than those without Svacancies[19]. Liu et al. reported the S-vacancies in $\mathsf{C o N i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ by the $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ reduction, and the reduced nanosheet delivers a high capacity of $1918\mathrm{F}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ [20]. Huang et al. produced the ${\mathsf{N i S@C}}$ hollow structure with S-vacancies, delivering a high reversible specific capacity $\ensuremath{1728\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}}$ , 1 $\mathfrak{A}\ g^{-1})[21]$ . Multiple strategies have been proposed to introduce the sulfur vacancies into metal sulfides, such as annealing in the reducing atmosphere and plasma exposure. While the complex procedures generating sulfur vacancies are less attractive for commercialization. Herein, we report a simple and efficient strategy to design and synthesize novel 2D nanosheets of P-doping CoS with S defects on CNT films to enhance the surface electrochemical activity. The density functional theory (DFT) proves that the introduction of doping and vacancy can improve the conductivity. The $\mathsf{P{-}C o S_{1-x}/C N T}$ electrode possesses a high capacity of $4.3\ensuremath{\mathrm{F}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cm}}^{-2}$ , good catalytic performance. In addition, the ASC based on $\mathrm{P-CoS_{1-X}//C o S@P P_{y}}$ exhibits excellent energy density $(0.18\mathrm{\mWh\cm^{-2}})$ and power density $(0.45\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}},$ . The electrocatalytic result suggests that the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ possesses the lowest overpotential of $330\mathrm{mV}$ and the smallest Tafel slope of $72.1\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ .
## 2. Experimental
2.1 Synthesis of CoS/CNT: 2 mmol $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(0.582~\mathrm{g})$ and 1 mmol ${\mathsf{C}}_{8}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ $_{(0.166\mathrm{g})}$ were dissolved in 28 mL N, N- dimethylformamide (DMF) with stirring, then $4~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol was dropped slowly into the solution with continuous stirring for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Later, $4\mathrm{mmol}$ thioacetamide was added, and ultrasound for 10 s. Next, the mixture solution was transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave with a $3\times4$ cm CNT film at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ After natural cooling to room temperature, the as-prepared samples were washed with deionized water and absolute ethanol three times and dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ overnight. For comparison, the CoS/CNT samples were subjected by a similar sulfide strategy at the different amounts of S source (1 mmol, 2.6 mmol and $6\mathrm{mmol}$ ), and the products were denoted as CoS-1, CoS-2.6, CoS-6, respectively.
## 2.1. synthesis of P-CoS/CNT
## 2.2. synthesis of $P{-}C o S_{\imath-x}/C N T$
$0.06\ g\mathrm{P-CoS_{1-x}/C N T}$ was soaked in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.5\mathrm{~M~NaBH_{4}}$ aqueous solution at room temperature without stirring for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the final $\mathrm{P-CoS_{1-x}/C N T}$ .
## 2.3. synthesis of CoS@PPy/CNT
An electrochemical deposition process was used to deposit $\mathsf{P P y}$ on CoS/CNT. First, an aqueous solution containing $\mathsf{N a C l O}_{4}(1.68\ \mathrm{g})$ , pyrrole $3~\mathrm{mL}$ monomer and deionized water $(57~\mathrm{mL})$ was configured. Then, a three-electrode configuration was used, in which the CoS/CNT was used as the working electrode, $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ electrode as the reference electrode, and platinum plate electrode as the counter electrode. Later, electrochemical deposition was carried out in the prepared aqueous solution above, the voltage was set at $0.8{\mathrm{~V}}_{:}$ and the deposition time was $500~s.$ . Finally, CoS@PPy/CNT electrode material was obtained by overnight drying at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
## 2.4. material characterization
The synthesized samples were characterized by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) and $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The morphology and structure of the samples were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) of the samples were measured on a Shimadzu UV-3600 NIR spectrophotometer to detect absorption within $200{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ . The electrochemical properties of the samples are evaluated by cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD), electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS).
## 2.5. Three-electrode electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical performances of the electrode materials were first measured in a three-electrode system with 3 M KOH electrolyte solution. A platinum plate electrode and a saturated
calomel electrode (SCE) were used as the counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively.
## 2.6. preparation of the P-CoS1-x/CNT//CoS@PPy/CNT asymmetric supercapacitor
A PVA/KOH gel electrolyte was prepared by mixing PVA $(3\ {\tt g})$ and KOH $(4.63\ \mathrm{g})$ with deionized water $(30~\mathrm{mL})$ and heating to $95~^{\circ}C$ under vigorous stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The electrodes were coated with the PVA/KOH. After dried, the PVA/KOH coated $\mathrm{P-CoS_{1-x}/C N T}$ and CoS@PPy/CNT electrodes were placed in parallel, followed by coating with PVA/KOH again to produce a solid-state flexible ASC device.
## 3. Results and discussion
In this work, CNT film is used as the substrate for all subsequent preparations. Fig. 1a illustrates the synthesis process of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1}$ $\textbf{x}$ composite on CNT film. Firstly, CoS nanosheets were formed on the CNT through a hydrothermal method. Then, a phosphorization process was adopted for the conversion of CoS into P-doped CoS (PCoS). This was realized by annealing the CoS nanosheets under flowing Ar using ${\sf N a H}_{2}{\sf P O}_{2}$ as the $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ source. Finally, the S defect PCoS $(\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}})$ are obtained by soaking the P-CoS nanosheets in $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ solution at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As shown in Fig. 1- b-e and Fig. S1-S4 (Supporting Information), both of the Co-MOF and CoS- $\cdot n$ (n denotes the content of thioacetamide (TAA), $n=2.6$ mmol, 4 mmol and $6\mathrm{mmol}$ ) are uniform and flexible 2D nanosheet with smooth surface connected with each other. As the content increased to 6 mmol, the nanosheets accumulated seriously (Fig. S2, Supporting Information). The SEM images of the S vacancy defect CoS $\mathrm{\mathop{CoS}}_{1-\mathrm{x}})$ and P-CoS are exhibited in Fig. S5-S6 (Supporting Information). Fig. 1e demonstrates that the shape of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ samples is a nanosheet with a thin characteristic and presents no significant change in the overall shape after the reduction treatment. The TEM image of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ reveals that the nanosheet is comprised of small nanoparticles accumulating with each other to form a mesoporous structure (Fig. 1g), which can provide an enlarged surface area, a large number of highly exposed electrochemically active sites and is beneficial to the transport of electrolyte during the electrochemical process. From the HRTEM image of the $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}},$ the clear lattice fringe spacing of $0.254~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in Fig. 1h–j match well with the (110) plane of the CoS, consistent with the XRD results. Furthermore, the visible discontinuous lattice fringes and dislocation suggest the generation of the S vacancies after the reduction treatment, which may facilitate the follow redox reactions. The in situ introduced atomic vacancies and defects of the electrode materials can enhance the electronic conductivity and provide more active sites for ion adsorption. The elemental mapping analysis of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ further illustrates the uniform distribution of P, Co, S and C elements in the superstructure (Fig. 1k).
The crystallographic structures, phase components, and surface properties of the samples were further investigated by the X-ray diffraction (XRD), electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) techniques. For the XRD patterns, all diffraction peaks could be assigned to CoS phase (JCPDS No. 03–065-8977) except one peak at $\sim55^{\circ}$ $\mathrm{\backslash}\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ , JCPDS No. 00–019-0364) (Fig. 2a). The diffraction peaks at $31.0^{\circ}$ $35.7^{\circ}$ , $47.1^{\circ}$ , $54.8^{\circ}$ , corresponding to (1 0 0), (1 0 1), (10 2), (1 1 0) planes, respectively. With the phosphorization and further reduction treatment, all diffraction peaks intensity gradually weaker. However, the phase is not affected by the introduction of P heteroatoms and S vacancies[17,21]. Moreover, according to Bragg’s law, $d=n$ k/2sinh, the (1 0 2) diffraction peak of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ and P-CoS samples shift to lower angles, implying expansion of the unit cell after the introduction of P heteroatoms and S defects[22]. This can be due to the atomic radius of P is larger than that of S, and the vacancies also result in an increase of interatomic distances[23–24]. Furthermore, compare with $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ the (1 0 2) diffraction peak of PCoS shift significantly reduced, which could be due to its surface oxidized P[17]. To further probe the localized S vacancy, EPR was performed (Fig. 2b). Compared with CoS, the characteristic signal of S vacancy for $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ is more intensified and broadened, suggesting the significantly increased vacancy content[25]. To further prove the existence of defects, the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) was used to measure $S/$ Co molar ratio. The $S/C0$ molar ratio in $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ is 0.79, which is smaller than 0.91 in P-CoS (Table. S1). The Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET) analysis has been performed to investigate the surface area of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ nanosheets. It displays a mesoporous structure with typical IV nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms with a BET surface area of $38.7~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 2c) compared with $19.83~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of CoS (Fig. S7, Supporting Information). Fig. 2d–f depict the XPS survey spectrum of $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}}.$ For the Co 2p spectra, the peaks at 779.3 and $794.5\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ , and the peaks at 782.5 and $798.7\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to $C0^{2+}$ . The other two peaks at 787.5 and $803.9\mathrm{eV}$ could be assigned Co $\mathsf{2p}$ satellite peaks[17,26– 28]. Furthermore, a negative shift (shift to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ of the Co 2p peaks was found for $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}},$ P-CoS, compared to CoS, implying the generation of $C0^{2+}$ during phosphorization and reduction processes [17]. Based on the peak area of $C0^{3+}$ and $C0^{2+}$ , the ratio of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/$ ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ is the highest for $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ among $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-x}},$ P-CoS and CoS, due to the presence of the maximum number of defect sites. In the S 2p spectra, it is rewarding to note that the $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}$ spectra of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ shifts to high binding energies in comparison to that of pristine CoS. The peak located at $161.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to the S $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , which is attributed to the metal-S bond[17,27–28]. And the peak at $162.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to S $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , it could be attributed to the S sites with low coordination, which is ascribed to the S vacancy[17,25]. The proportion of $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ in $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ increases relative to P-CoS and CoS, demonstrating the newly formed S vacancy in $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathrm{x}}[25]$ . This is probably due to the partial substitution of P for S and the formation of surface S defects[17]. For the $\mathtt{P2p}$ spectra, the $\mathtt{P2p}$ peaks are observed indicating the existence of P, while no $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ peak can be seen in CoS. In $\mathtt{P2p}$ region, two peaks at 129.6 (P $\cdot2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\mathrm{,\d}$ and 131.5 eV $(\mathsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ are attributed to the metal-P bonding after doping[29]. Another peak at $133.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to the PAO[29], which possibly comes from the oxidized $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ during air exposure. It can be divided into three peaks, which are assigned to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ $.136.2\ \mathrm{eV};$ , $\mathsf{H P O}_{4}^{2-}$ $(135.3~\mathrm{eV})$ , and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ ( $.134.4~\mathrm{eV})$ . The peak corresponds to $\mathsf{P}{-}0$ of P-CoS after reduction processes, which is due to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{H P O}_{4}^{2-}$ reacting with $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ and converting to $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}[17]$ . These results suggest that the $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ heteroatoms have been successfully doped into CoS lattice by phosphorization.
The electronic structures of CoS and $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}}$ were studied using density functional theory (DFT) simulations. As expected, the P$\mathsf{C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ exhibits the stronger metallic characteristic due to the continuous electronic state at Fermi level (Fig. 3a&c). The calculation results suggest that the metallic characteristic induced by P doping and S vacancy are conducive to modulating and ameliorating the electronic conductivity[30], the results are consistent with the CV results (Fig. 3e). Excellent conductivity benefits to the transmission of electrons, which is the main reason for the high electrochemical properties of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ . Based on the XRD results, the structural model of CoS and $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ are built, which are shown in Fig. 3b&d. The UV–vis DRS of the samples are given in Fig. S8, (Supporting Information). Based on the corresponding Tauc plots from the Kubelka-Munk function via plotting the $(a h v)^{2}$ -ht relationship in Fig. 3f, the bandgap $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\mathtt{g}}\right)$ of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ is declined to about $2.39\mathrm{eV}$ than pure CoS (2.51 eV) and P-CoS (2.59 eV), revealing the shallowing band gap impact and P doping and sulfur vacancy-induced varied electronic structure[25]. More precisely, the P doping and generated sulfur vacancies promote the formation of donor energy states below the conduction band, narrow acceptor states surrounding the valence band, and then reduce the band gap[25,31– 32].
The electrochemical behaviors of the samples in 3 M KOH electrolyte were evaluated using a three-electrode system by CV, GCD and EIS. To determine the appropriate adding of TAA, three different proportions of CoS- $\cdot n$ and Co-MOF samples are prepared and electrochemical tested (Fig. S9-13, Supporting Information). When the TAA adding is $4\ \mathrm{mmol}$ , the CoS has the highest capacitance (Fig. S14, Supporting Information). When the concentrate and soaking time of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ is $0.5\mathrm{{M}}$ and $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ electrode exhibits the highest electrochemical properties (Fig. S15-16). To understand the role of $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ dopants and S vacancies in the charge storage performance, the CV curves of CoS, P-CoS, $\mathsf{C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ and $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ electrodes were collected at a scan rate of $30\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ with an operating voltage window of $-0.5$ to $0.5\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. 3e). All electrodes deliver two pairs of redox peaks, demonstrating good pseudocapacitance behavior. The CV area of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ is larger than others, suggesting the $\mathrm{\bf\Pi}_{\mathrm{{{P}}}}$ doping and S vacancies can obtain superior capacitance. CV curves of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ under different scan rates were shown in
Fig. 3g. The corresponding redox reaction can be attributed to the reversible faradaic redox processes of $\mathsf C0^{2+}/\mathsf C0^{3+}/\mathsf C0^{4+}$ with $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ in the electrolyte:
$$
\mathrm{CoS\+\0H^{-}\longleftrightarrow}\mathrm{CoSOH\+\e^{-}}
$$
$$
{\mathsf{C o S O H}}+{\mathsf{O H}}^{-}\longleftrightarrow{\mathsf{C o S O}}+{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}+{\mathsf{e}}^{-}
$$
It is well known that the charge storage consists of two parts: diffusion-controlled reaction and surface-controlled capacitance. The contribution of the two parts capacitance was estimated according to the formulas: $i=a\nu^{b}$ , where $a$ and $b$ are variables. A $b$ value of 1 suggests a capacitive process and 0.5 is indicative of diffusion-controlled charge storage[33]. With scan rate increased from 1 to $5{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ , the $b$ values of peaks 1 to 4 are 0.53, 0.74, 0.54 and 0.86, respectively (Fig. S17, Supporting Information), indicating the electrochemical reactions are controlled by both surface-controlled capacitance and diffusion-limited redox reactions[34]. In Fig. 3h, the pink-shaded regions represent the contribution from the surface capacitance of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ nanosheets at $1\mathrm{~mV~}s^{-1}$ . Fig. 3i shows the histograms for comparison of the capacity ratio of diffusion-controlled and surface capacitance for $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ at $1{-}5\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . With increasing the scan rate, the effective surface charge storage improved. The GCD curves of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ nanosheets are shown in Fig. S18, Supporting Information. The GCD curves at different currents are nearly symmetric, indicating high reversibility of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ electrode[35]. Moreover, as the current increased from 0.4 to $5~\mathrm{mA}$ , the capacitance dropped from 4.3 to $3.1\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , with a retention rate of $72\%$ , indicating the good rate performance of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ electrode. The electrode performance is better than other reports (Table S2).
The SEM image in Fig. 4a displays that the CoS@PPy composite holds a disk-like 3D layered architecture with an average diameter of $0.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Raman spectroscopy was carried out to confirm the presence of $\mathsf{P P y}$ in Fig. 4b. The characteristic peaks at 935, 980, 1048, 1374 and $1595~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the functional groups of PPy. The peak at 1595 and $1374~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the ringstretching mode of PPy. The peak at $1048~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ in-plane deformation, and the peaks at 935 and $980~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ backbone stretching and ring-in plane deformation of $\mathsf{P P y}$ , respectively, indicating the successful deposition of PPy[36]. The detailed electrochemical properties of the $\mathsf{C o S@P P y}$ negative electrode were also analyzed in Fig. 4c–d. The CoS@PPy electrode shows pseudocapacitive characteristics over $-1\sim0\:\mathsf{V}$ with a pair of redox peaks of CV curves (Fig. 4c). When the scan rate increased from 10 to $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , the shapes of the CV curves for the $\mathsf{C o S@P P y}$ electrode were maintained, indicating good electrochemical stability. Fig. 4d shows the GCD curves at different currents ranging from 1 to $5\mathrm{mA}$ . The calculated capacity of ${\mathsf{C o S@P P y}}$ electrode is 2.05, 2.3, 2.4 and $2.55\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the current of 5, 3, 2 and $1~\mathrm{mA}$ , respectively.
To evaluate the feasibility of the obtained electrodes for practical application, the ASC was assembled with $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ and $\mathsf{C o S@P P y}$ as the positive and negative electrodes, respectively, the PVA/KOH as the flexible electrolyte, as illustrated in Fig. 5a. To optimized the performance of the final ASC, the CV curves at $30\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ of the electrodes were firstly characterized by a three-electrode cell in
Fig. 5b to achieve the charges balance $(Q^{+}{=}Q^{-})$ . On the basis of the equation $Q=C V,$ , where C is the specific capacitance, and $\mathsf{V}$ is the voltage range. According to relevant dates derived from Fig. 5b, we summarized the optimal area ratio between the P$\mathsf{C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ and ${\mathsf{C o S@P P y}}$ electrodes should be about 1:1.9 in the full ASC. The overall specific capacitance of the ASC device is come to form the combined contribution of EDLC-type capacitance and Faradaic pseudocapacitance, simultaneously (Fig. 5c). The GCD curves are shown in Fig. 5d, based on the corresponding discharging time, the areal specific capacitance is calculated to be $410\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Fig. 5e). Fig. 5f presents the corresponding Ragone plot of the ASC, the device delivered a high energy density of $0.18\mathrm{\mWh\cm^{-2}}$ at a high power density of $0.45\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [37]. The x-intercept of the Nyquist plots represents the equivalent series resistance (ESR) and the charge transport resistance. After cycling for 4000 cycles, the device demonstrated a high stability and a little high ESR (Fig. 5g–h). The ASC retains 90, 96.4, 93.3, and $103.7\%$ of its original capacitance after bending for 45, 90, 135, and $180^{\circ}$ , respectively (Fig. 5i), proving the mechanical stability of our ASC.
The electrochemical activity of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathrm{x}}$ toward OER was measured in a three-electrode system with 1 M KOH as the electrolyte. LSV curves of catalysts concerning OER are plotted in Fig. 6a. Compared to CoS, P-CoS, $\mathsf{C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ and $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ have lower overpotential at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Among them, $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ exhibits the lowest overpotential of $330~\mathrm{mV}$ . This result suggests that the incorporation of P doping and vacancies can significantly improve the OER activity [38]. Apart from that, Tafel plots (Fig. 6b) determined from LSVs were used to understand the OER kinetics. As observed, $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ possesses the smallest Tafel slope of $72.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , confirming its outstanding OER kinetics process. EIS measurements were carried out under the potential of $634\mathrm{mV}$ to uncover the origin of the outstanding catalytic activity of $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-x}},$ corresponding Nyquist plots are demonstrated in Fig. 6d. As observed, the electrolyte resistance $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ is almost constant[39], and all the materials exhibit two different semicircles. The high-frequency semicircle corresponds to the impedance of the substrate and material $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct(int)}})$ . And in low-frequency region, the semicircle corresponds to the impedance of the material and electrolyte $\left(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct(solid-liquid)}}\right),$ . Obviously, $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ exhibits the lowest charge-transfer resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}})_{\cdot}$ , suggesting the fastest charge transfer during OER[40]. The enhancement in the electron transfer kinetics is mainly attributed to the improved electrical conductivity of the $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ induced by structural defects of $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ doping and vacancies[41]. To obtain the double-layer capacitance $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ value, CV curves in a non-Faradaic potential range of 1.172–1.272 V vs. RHE were recorded (Fig. S19, Supporting Information), and the capacitance values were calculated by plotting the current density at $1.177\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE against the various scan rates. In Fig. 6e, the $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values were found to be 35.0, 29.6, 24.9, and $13.8~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ for $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1-\mathrm{x}},$ $\mathsf{C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}},$ P-CoS and CoS, respectively. The highest electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) of $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ among the samples suggests that more accessible active sites for electrochemical reactions are exposed. It is beneficial for obtaining the high electrocatalytic activity[42]. On the other hand, in Fig. 6f, $\mathsf{P-C o S}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ shows stable potential retention at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ during 80000 s for chronopotentiometric response.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have designed P-doping CoS with S defect 2D nanosheet through solvothermal and pyrolytic phosphating and further demonstrated the impact of $\mathsf{P}$ -doping and S defects for improving the electrochemical performance of the CoS electrode for SCs. Furthermore, the doping and defect have promoted the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performance of CoS. The electrocatalytic result suggests that the $\mathrm{P-CoS}_{1\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}}$ possesses the lowest overpotential and the smallest Tafel slope. The fabricated ASC exhibits a high energy density of $0.18\mathrm{\mWh\cm^{-2}}$ at a high power density of $0.45\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ with an output voltage of $1.8\mathrm{~V~}$ , as well as excellent bending performance. These results demonstrated that the fabricated electrode from doping and defect engineering can be used for next generation high performance flexible SC and other energy devices.
